Option-A scaffold for the Plantard NTT optimisation of HAWK-512 sign:
no behaviour change to the build, just the analysis and the empirical
search infrastructure to lock down the encoding before any production
code lands.

PLANTARD_NOTES.md captures:
  * Why the optimisation target is hawk_sign.c only (15 mq18433_*
    call sites at Q=18433). hawk_vrfy.c and ng_mp31.c use 31-bit
    primes outside Plantard's q < 2^15 sweet spot.
  * Derived Plantard constants for Q=18433:
      Qinv_Plant = 339720193   (q^{-1} mod 2^32; q * Qinv = 1)
      qa         = +1          (rounding correction, since q < 2^15)
  * The open math question: HAWK keeps NTT coefficients in single-
    Montgomery form (x * R mod q, R = 2^16), Plantard naturally
    produces x * 2^{-32} mod q. Either rebake GM/iGM tables so the
    Plantard product lands back in single-Mont, or switch HAWK's
    representation. We will pursue table rebaking — the calibration
    test searches the encoding space empirically.
  * HAWK spec adherence: outputs must be BYTE-identical to the
    reference Montgomery routines, not merely modulo-equivalent, so
    sign / verify stay byte-equal to the round-2 KAT vectors.

tests/test_plantard_calibrate.c (option-A search harness):
  * Pulls in HAWK's reference Montgomery routines as the golden
    oracle (mq18433_montymul).
  * Enumerates 2 (sign of Qinv) x 4 (twiddle pre-encodings)
    candidate Plantard formulations and runs each against 4096
    random (a, b) pairs in [1..Q].
  * Reports per-combo mismatch count and the first failing input,
    so the next iteration can extend the candidate set until a
    combo matches every input.

Build: 'make -C crypto_sign/hawk512/m4/tests run-calibrate'.
None of the eight initial candidates currently match — by design;
this commit is the scaffold, not the resolution. The follow-up
commit adds the winning combination (or new candidates if none of
the obvious ones win) and ships plant_18433.{h,c}.
